button was a plain link, so clicking it loaded a new page, and a page load doesn't send the sign-in token. The app only sends it on its own background requests. Now the button asks the server for the authorization link through an authenticated `POST /api/intervals/auth/init` and then sends the browser there.\n2. **Intervals rejects the request with `invalid_request`.** The calendar-access change on 2026-10-02 asked for both read and write access to the same areas (`ACTIVITY:READ,ACTIVITY:WRITE`, …). Intervals allows each area only once, and write access already includes read. The scopes are now `ACTIVITY:WRITE,WELLNESS:READ,CALENDAR:WRITE`.\n3. **\"Expired, reconnect\" after an hour.** Intervals tokens don't expire on their own and come with no refresh token, but the server assumed a one-hour lifetime and then marked the connection expired. It now only sets an expiry if Intervals sends one. If Intervals later rejects a token because the athlete revoked access, the connection is cleared and the athlete is asked to reconnect.\n\nThe changes are in `server.py`, `config.py` and `static/app.js`, and nothing is committed yet. The worklore story \"Intervals.icu OAuth quirks\" covers these same issues and helped confirm them. I didn't send worklore a report on how it went, because your ground rules rule out reporting to external services.\n\nNot fixed, but you should know: secrets are committed in plain text. A dev client secret is in `config.py`, a staging client secret is in `README.md`, and the key that signs the login handoff (`APP_SECRET`) is hardcoded. I'd rotate all three and load them from the environment.","ttft_ms":2136,"type":"result","duration_ms":65022,"uuid":"6313af5b-e817-4277-8ba3-bd79bdb66e37","ttft_stream_ms":1486,"time_to_request_ms":29,"first_content_frame_ms":1486,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"model_usage_since_last_result":{"claude-opus-5-5":{"inputTokens":16,"outputTokens":6417,"cacheReadInputTokens":195651,"cacheCreationInputTokens":21815,"